  "body": "Stacked on #2981. Review the [changes above the parent](https://github.com/qsmits/Camcraft/compare/1e12c77a4150252c34df3f23fc2ff1c85fe2e3fa...13c98065810173c6da65264cfa6799df470d5cd6).\n\nThis PR is ported onto the zero-step setup frame of #2984 and the stack up to #2981. The old writer of this PR is gone. The old head was `c81cfd1d4`.\n\n## What changed\n\n- A punch group holds 2 to 9 complete modeled punches on one 20 mm plate. The limit is `agie_setup::MAX_PUNCH_WORKS` (9). It applies in these three places:\n  - `PunchGroup20::encode` (was `GROUP_WORKS = 2`);\n  - `agie::export_group_with_review` (was `jobs.len() != 2`);\n  - the app dispatch in `posting_agie.rs` (was `1..=2`).\n- Each work has its own ISO (`<group>1.ISO` to `<group>9.ISO`), its own SBR line, its own work `CUTn` and its own threading hole. All works refer to the ONE `CCB030` quality object of the piece (#2984). The old PR body said \"each work gets its own resident quality object\". This port does not do that, because #2984 showed that different quality names can bind to different resident data.\n- The clear-band check compares every pair of works, not only neighbours in the program. The message names the two works, for example `The works TRIO201 and TRIO203 share a cutting area`.\n- A punch group refuses a placed drawing, as a die group does.\n- Die groups stay at two openings. The PR has no control evidence for larger die groups. `encode_die`, the post and the app refuse a third opening with their own message.\n- New messages: `SetupError::GroupWorks`, the post group refusal and the app count refusal state the new limits.\n- New examples `examples/agie/punch-trio.camscript` and `punch-grid.camscript`. They use the post name `Native wire EDM job files`.\n- The user guide section is now **Group captured punches**.\n\n## Value sources\n\nNo new control value. The 20 mm offsets (245, 155, 140 and 134 µm), the TECCUT row and all SBL fields are those of the checked pair `PAIR20` (#2961). Only the work name, the resource and the hole change for each work. The stock (120 × 100 × 20 mm), the 32 mm pitch, the 14 × 16 mm punch (`pair-left.obj`) and the holes 13 mm left of each centre are from the examples of this PR.\n\n## Readings\n\nEmulated Classic Cut 2S, 2026-11-24, throwaway clone of the practice image (base SHA-256 unchanged, clone deleted). The files of this frame were imported, and the bytes on the guest disk were identical.\n\n| | TRIO20 (3 works) | NINE20 (9 works) |\n|---|---|---|\n| After import | `CCB030[0]`, own `stp1` and ISO on each work | same, 9 distinct resources |\n| Quality on each work | CCB030 Ra 0.30, Tkm 1.0, Te 1.5, options off | same, also on CUT9 |\n| TECCUT | row 0199, `estbca25nnn060b030020`, T100 on each work (20 mm row) | same |\n| After TECCUT | `CCB030[4]` on each work | same |\n| FORMALCHECK | `geen fouten`; warnings `Uitgekozen opties : 131072`, `Referentiewaarden : te definiëren` | same |\n| J00 cut blocks | 24 | 72 |\n| Cut order | profile passes of CUT1..CUT3, then the 1.000 mm separations | profile passes of CUT1..CUT9, then the separations |\n| Transits between works | head up (Z53 or Z80) only | same, 37 moves, none below Z53 |\n| Cutting envelopes | 3 pairs, 0 overlap | 36 pairs, 0 overlap |\n\nThe quality problem of the old run (the first and the ninth generic quality names bound to different resident targets) does not occur: there is only one quality object.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- `camcraft-post` lib: `group_count_tests::every_group_size_has_its_own_file_work_and_hole_and_one_quality`, `a_tenth_punch_or_a_third_die_opening_writes_nothing`, `a_collision_of_works_that_are_not_neighbours_names_the_pair`; `group_tests::a_group_refuses_a_die_two_works_on_one_area_another_stock_or_no_separation` (updated).\n- `camcraft-post` `agie_group_setup`: `trio_and_grid_files_match_their_goldens_with_one_quality` (new goldens `TRIO20.SBL/SBR`, `NINE20.SBL/SBR`), `a_group_refuses_its_unqualified_inputs` (ten works); `agie_die_group_setup`: three distinct openings refused.\n- `camcraft-app` `agie_native`: `group::three_model_owners_post_the_trio_goldens` (ISO goldens `TRIO201..203.ISO`), `group::nine_model_owners_post_the_grid_goldens_with_their_own_iso_and_hole`, `group::a_tenth_work_an_unowned_model_a_shared_source_or_a_far_collision_writes_nothing`, `die_group::a_missing_shared_or_unsafe_die_work_writes_nothing` (three die works).\n- Unchanged bytes: the PAIR20, DUODI20, CCSEP20, DIE20, LOWER20, RACE20, CIRCLE20, FINE20 and inspection goldens are not edited, and their tests pass.\n\n## Gate\n\n`cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` on `13c980658` (the stack touches geometry, so quick falls back to batch): 1193 steps, 1192 ok, 0 stalled. The step `clippy -D warnings` (geometry, cl, tools, post, sim, motion, app, gpu, ui, all targets) passed. The one red step is `app suites: rotary_verify` (`a_rough_over_an_oversized_bar_peels_from_the_stock_radius`). That is the known red main of #3774 on the stack base; this PR does not touch that code. No heavy lane, by AGENTS.md (`P6`).\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No physical cut.\n- A tenth work, mixed punch and die groups, and die groups of more than two openings: refused, no control run.\n- Nested or diagonal layouts that a box check refuses although they are safe.\n\nChangelog: The native wire EDM post accepts groups of up to nine captured punches on one 20 mm plate. It checks the cutting areas of every pair of works and names the two works that meet. All works use one quality object.\n\nFixes #2980\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
